<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/4th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Corinthian War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/395-bce">395</a>–387 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Corinthian War was fought from 395 to 387 BCE between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> and an alliance of Thebes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, Corinth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a>, supported at first by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. Sparta won on land at Nemea and Coronea but lost its fleet at Cnidus (394 BCE); the war ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-antalcidas">King's Peace</a> of 387 BCE, dictated by the Persian king, which returned the Greek cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> to Persia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/corinthian-war.jpg" alt="Corinthian War" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tilemahos Efthimiadis from Athens, Greece · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grave_relief_of_Dexileos,_son_of_Lysanias,_of_Thorikos_(Ca._390_BC)_(4454389225).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> ended in 404 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> alone received the plunder and the tribute of the former Athenian Empire, which alienated its allies. In 402 BCE Sparta attacked Elis, and Corinth and Thebes refused to send troops. Thebes, Corinth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> also refused to join a Spartan expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a> in 398 BCE, in which King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agesilaus-ii">Agesilaus II</a> campaigned as far inland as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>. In 395 BCE the Thebans encouraged their allies the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locrians">Locrians</a> to collect taxes from disputed territory, which led to fighting with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocis">Phocis</a>, an ally of Sparta. Sparta mobilised, and Athens voted to support <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a>. In late 395 BCE Corinth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> joined, and the allies' council at Corinth gave the war its name. Sparta recalled Agesilaus from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>At Nemea in 394 BCE the Spartans won a decisive victory, in which the coalition lost 2,800 men and the Spartans and their allies 1,100. At Coronea in the same year both right wings were victorious, the Thebans breaking through before Agesilaus met them head on. At sea Agesilaus had raised 120 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">triremes</a> under his brother-in-law Peisander, but the Persian fleet, commanded by the satrap <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharnabazus-ii">Pharnabazus II</a> and the Athenian admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conon">Conon</a>, destroyed it at Cnidus, which effectively ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>'s attempts to become a naval power.</p><p>Pharnabazus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conon">Conon</a> then captured Spartan-allied cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a> and in 393 BCE based their fleet on Melos, the first time in 90 years that a Persian fleet had gone so far west. In 391 BCE the Athenian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iphicrates">Iphicrates</a>, with light troops and peltasts, defeated a Spartan regiment at Lechaeum, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> and Corinth were merged. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> recaptured a number of islands, and Thrasybulus won over states in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> before he was killed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Alarmed by Athenian successes, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> stopped backing the allies. Antalcidas arranged with the satrap Tiribazus that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> would join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> if the allies refused peace, and his fleet threatened the grain route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-antalcidas">King's Peace</a> of 387 BCE was dictated by Artaxerxes II: Persia was to control <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a>, all other Greek cities were to be autonomous, which in effect barred leagues and alliances, and Sparta was to enforce the terms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> was the main loser, as the Boeotian League was disbanded and its cities garrisoned by Sparta.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> by 380 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> used the autonomy clause to break up coalitions, splitting Mantinea into five villages. War between Sparta and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> resumed in 378 BCE and Spartan dominance ended at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra">Leuctra</a> in 371 BCE. Athens, with walls and fleet restored, began to recover power and formed the Second Athenian League.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Corinthian War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the <b>Corinthian War</b> Boeotian forces killed the Spartan commander Lysander at the Battle of Haliartus in 395 BCE and fought Agesilaus II at the Battle of Coronea in 394 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its rule through harmosts and narrow oligarchies alienated former allies, and in the <b>Corinthian War</b> of 395–387 BCE Thebes, Athens, Corinth and Argos fought Sparta with Persian support, while a Persian fleet destroyed the Spartan navy at Cnidus in 394 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Corinth fought Sparta in the <b>Corinthian War</b> of 395 to 387 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/corinth">Corinth</a></li></ul></section>
